# Network

This document outlines the network configuration used in the home lab.

## Network Layout
- Host machine connected to home router
- VirtualBox provides virtual networking to guest VMs
- pfSense VM used as a virtual firewall/router
- Two main virtual networks:
  1. NAT (for internet access via pfSense)
  2. Host-Only network (for internal VM communication)

## VirtualBox Networking Mode
- NAT Network (through pfSense):
  - Purpose: Provides internet access to VMs securely via pfSense firewall
  - Allows controlled port forwarding
  - Used for external connectivity while keeping VMs isolated from the host network

- Host-Only Network:
  - Purpose: Isolated network for VMs to communicate with each other and the host
  - No direct internet access
  - Useful for lab testing and service communication

## IP Addressing
- IP addresses assigned via pfSense DHCP for NAT network
- IP addresses assigned via VirtualBox DHCP for Host-Only network
- All IPs shown in documentation are sanitized

Example (sanitized):
- Host: 192.168.x.x
- pfSense WAN (NAT): 192.168.x.x
- pfSense LAN (Host-Only): 10.0.x.x
- VM (Host-Only): 10.0.x.x

## Connectivity
- VMs can access the internet through the pfSense NAT
- Host can access VMs on the Host-Only network
- Internal services can communicate securely without exposing them to the host network
- Port forwarding configured on pfSense for external service access

## Future Improvements
